 "key_sub": "aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ and a⁰ = 1.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>An <b>exponent</b> tells how many times the base is multiplied: 2<sup>5</sup> = 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 32.</p><p>Laws: multiply → add the exponents; divide → subtract; power of a power → multiply. a<sup>0</sup> = 1 and a<sup>−n</sup> = 1/a<sup>n</sup>.</p><p><b>Scientific notation</b> writes a number as a × 10<sup>n</sup> with 1 ≤ a &lt; 10, such as 3,40,000 = 3.4 × 10<sup>5</sup>.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Simplify 3<sup>4</sup> × 3<sup>5</sup>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Same base: add the exponents</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 3<sup>9</sup></p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Write 0.00052 in scientific notation.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Move the point 4 places right</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 5.2 × 10<sup>−4</sup></p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Working out 2<sup>5</sup> as 2 × 5 = 10.</li><li>Multiplying the exponents in a<sup>3</sup> × a<sup>4</sup> (it is a<sup>7</sup>, not a<sup>12</sup>).</li><li>Thinking 4<sup>−2</sup> is negative. It is 1/16.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What are the laws of exponents?",
   "a": "aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ, aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ, a⁰ = 1 and a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is scientific notation?",
   "a": "A way to write very large or small numbers as a × 10ⁿ, where 1 ≤ a < 10 and n is an integer."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why is any number to the power 0 equal to 1?",
   "a": "Because aⁿ ÷ aⁿ = 1, and by the quotient law it is also a⁰."
  }
 ],
